WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.940
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.287
Building the Attribute-Driven Migration.

3
00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:10.162
This is Chapter 2: Graph clients
for the government clouds.

4
00:00:10.162 --> 00:00:15.558
Every piece of code in this workbook that
calls Microsoft Graph should follow one

5
00:00:15.558 --> 00:00:20.717
pattern: know which cloud it's in, send its
token nowhere else, ask for no more than

6
00:00:20.717 --> 00:00:25.875
each call needs, and keep working when
Graph says slow down or a beta API changes.

7
00:00:25.875 --> 00:00:30.347
We'll sort out which cloud is which,
run Microsoft's own snippets to see

8
00:00:30.347 --> 00:00:34.975
what they leave out, walk the client's
rules, then two labs and the dangers.

9
00:00:34.975 --> 00:00:40.480
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:46.330
Why does this chapter matter so much?

11
00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:50.076
Because every other chapter's
code talks to Graph or to Azure,

12
00:00:50.076 --> 00:00:53.597
and a client that gets the cloud
wrong, or asks for too much,

13
00:00:53.597 --> 00:00:56.668
carries that mistake into
everything built on it.

14
00:00:56.668 --> 00:01:01.469
So the goal is a client that reaches the
right cloud, asks for the right token,

15
00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:06.580
makes only the calls it declares, and pages
and retries the way Microsoft says to.

16
00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:11.192
Just as important, for each rule in the
code you should be able to say whether

17
00:01:11.192 --> 00:01:15.580
it's Microsoft's contract, Microsoft's
sample, or the program's own design.

18
00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:17.580
Let's start with the clouds.

19
00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:25.005
Get this right first, because
every other rule depends on it.

20
00:01:25.005 --> 00:01:28.860
A GCC Moderate tenant uses
the worldwide endpoints,

21
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.805
the same as a commercial
tenant: Learn says so plainly.

22
00:01:32.805 --> 00:01:36.288
GCC was built when Microsoft
had one cloud directory,

23
00:01:36.288 --> 00:01:39.217
and it still uses that
commercial directory.

24
00:01:39.217 --> 00:01:44.116
It's GCC High and the Department of
Defense cloud that have their own hosts,

25
00:01:44.116 --> 00:01:47.168
for tokens, for Graph
and for Resource Manager.

26
00:01:47.168 --> 00:01:50.479
And a token from one cloud
simply doesn't work in another.

27
00:01:50.479 --> 00:01:52.394
The slide maps all four clouds,

28
00:01:52.394 --> 00:01:56.360
including the PowerShell
environment names you'll pass.

29
00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:04.016
The target estate for this
migration is Microsoft 365

30
00:02:04.016 --> 00:02:07.835
GCC with commercial Azure,
so let's make that concrete.

31
00:02:07.835 --> 00:02:11.197
Nothing in its configuration
is a dot-u-s host.

32
00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:15.495
A run that reads both Graph and
Resource Manager needs two tokens,

33
00:02:15.495 --> 00:02:18.997
because one token request
can only name one resource.

34
00:02:18.997 --> 00:02:23.234
The code still calls the cloud "gcc"
by name, even though it resolves

35
00:02:23.234 --> 00:02:27.548
to the worldwide endpoints, so the
evidence says what was intended.

36
00:02:27.548 --> 00:02:32.507
And watch for a second tenant: if a
workload ever lands in an Azure Government

37
00:02:32.507 --> 00:02:37.440
subscription, it has its own hosts, and
the run has to be told, never guess.

38
00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:44.628
This chapter is the reference
for clients that already exist.

39
00:02:44.628 --> 00:02:47.140
Chapters 1, 3 and 4 were written first,

40
00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:51.664
and each has its own Graph client
that follows part of the pattern.

41
00:02:51.664 --> 00:02:54.029
They name the clouds two different ways:

42
00:02:54.029 --> 00:02:58.040
the program's short names, and
Learn's PowerShell environment names.

43
00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:01.290
That's fine, because the
table maps one to the other.

44
00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.052
What isn't fine is a default.

45
00:03:03.052 --> 00:03:06.473
A cloud name a client doesn't
recognize has to stop the run,

46
00:03:06.473 --> 00:03:09.965
because quietly falling back
to worldwide is exactly how

47
00:03:09.965 --> 00:03:13.530
a government run ends up
talking to the wrong service.

48
00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:18.992
Here's what Microsoft gives you.

49
00:03:18.992 --> 00:03:23.973
Learn's page on national clouds for the
Graph SDKs names the three things a client

50
00:03:23.973 --> 00:03:26.798
has to change per cloud:
where it gets its token,

51
00:03:26.798 --> 00:03:30.292
where Graph lives, and any
scope that names the Graph host.

52
00:03:30.292 --> 00:03:33.092
Its example connects to GCC High.

53
00:03:33.092 --> 00:03:36.106
Other Learn pages cover
paging and throttling.

54
00:03:36.106 --> 00:03:41.056
The Python examples live in Microsoft's
snippets repository, so the workbook

55
00:03:41.056 --> 00:03:45.693
pins an unmodified copy of the two files
it uses, with a hash a test checks.

56
00:03:45.693 --> 00:03:49.274
A test then runs them against
stand-ins that record exactly

57
00:03:49.274 --> 00:03:52.790
what they'd sign in to,
without signing in to anything.

58
00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:00.397
Running the snippets against the
stand-ins shows what they leave to you,

59
00:04:00.397 --> 00:04:03.077
and the first half is about
cloud and credentials.

60
00:04:03.077 --> 00:04:05.348
The surprise is the Python credential:

61
00:04:05.348 --> 00:04:09.817
on the same page where the other languages
set the government authority, the

62
00:04:09.817 --> 00:04:14.640
Python example doesn't, and Python's identity
library defaults to the public cloud.

63
00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:18.865
Copy it for GCC High and the
app signs in to the wrong cloud.

64
00:04:18.865 --> 00:04:22.170
There's also nothing for the
Department of Defense cloud,

65
00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:25.616
a certificate snippet that
hard-codes the worldwide scope,

66
00:04:25.616 --> 00:04:29.000
and a client secret, which is
just a password in a file.

67
00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:35.237
The second half is about running safely.

68
00:04:35.237 --> 00:04:38.475
The snippets have no Resource
Manager endpoints at all.

69
00:04:38.475 --> 00:04:40.956
Nothing ties a call to
the permission it needs,

70
00:04:40.956 --> 00:04:44.613
though Learn asks for the least
privileged permission for each one.

71
00:04:44.613 --> 00:04:47.279
There's no paging in the Python snippets,

72
00:04:47.279 --> 00:04:52.612
the SDK won't retry throttled items inside
a batch, and there's nothing about beta.

73
00:04:52.612 --> 00:04:57.236
So the verdict: use Learn's page as the
model for the three things that change per

74
00:04:57.236 --> 00:05:01.502
cloud, add the authority, and for
background jobs use the hardened client,

75
00:05:01.502 --> 00:05:06.480
because a reviewer will ask which calls,
with which permissions, in which cloud.

76
00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:11.530
Now the code itself.

77
00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:14.446
Everything about a cloud
comes from one table:

78
00:05:14.446 --> 00:05:17.823
ask for a cloud by name and
you get its token authority,

79
00:05:17.823 --> 00:05:22.505
its Graph host and its Resource Manager
host, and anything else is an error.

80
00:05:22.505 --> 00:05:25.443
The client won't send its
token to any other host.

81
00:05:25.443 --> 00:05:28.493
Then there's the call list,
one entry per Graph call,

82
00:05:28.493 --> 00:05:33.405
with the least privileged permission from
the API page and the tasks that use it.

83
00:05:33.405 --> 00:05:36.707
Two rules follow: a task
can make only its own calls,

84
00:05:36.707 --> 00:05:40.493
and its permissions are exactly
the union of those calls.

85
00:05:40.493 --> 00:05:43.740
Add one without the other,
and the build fails.

86
00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:49.510
The third rule is the token check,

87
00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:52.428
and it runs before a live
job makes a single call.

88
00:05:52.428 --> 00:05:56.440
An app-only token lists its
application permissions in one claim,

89
00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:59.339
so the Python client reads
it from its own token.

90
00:05:59.339 --> 00:06:02.077
If a permission is missing, the run stops.

91
00:06:02.077 --> 00:06:04.840
If there's one the task
doesn't use, it warns,

92
00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:08.090
because an unused permission
is still exposure.

93
00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:11.124
PowerShell is trickier,
since the Graph SDK

94
00:06:11.124 --> 00:06:14.327
signs in for you and never
hands over the token.

95
00:06:14.327 --> 00:06:18.020
It turns out the session's context
reports the same permissions,

96
00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:21.720
read from the same claim, so
the module checks that instead.

97
00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:28.970
Paging and throttling are
where clients quietly break.

98
00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:32.608
When Graph returns a next link,
the client follows it to the end,

99
00:06:32.608 --> 00:06:36.887
and it sends the same headers on every
page, because Learn says they aren't

100
00:06:36.887 --> 00:06:41.095
carried forward by default, and an
advanced query changes without them.

101
00:06:41.095 --> 00:06:45.207
When Graph says slow down, the
client waits as long as it's told.

102
00:06:45.207 --> 00:06:49.396
When it isn't told, which really
happens for Conditional Access,

103
00:06:49.396 --> 00:06:50.846
it backs off, with a cap.

104
00:06:50.846 --> 00:06:56.220
It also marks its requests as low priority,
so background reads give way to people.

105
00:06:56.220 --> 00:07:00.520
And inside a batch, it retries
throttled items itself.

106
00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:09.807
Beta APIs can change or disappear, and
Learn says not to use them in production.

107
00:07:09.807 --> 00:07:13.102
But sometimes the only place
a property lives is beta,

108
00:07:13.102 --> 00:07:16.021
like the Autopilot status
this workbook needs.

109
00:07:16.021 --> 00:07:17.852
So beta calls must be declared,

110
00:07:17.852 --> 00:07:21.809
they're read through a wrapper that
turns a missing shape into "not

111
00:07:21.809 --> 00:07:26.133
evaluated" rather than a failed run,
and the stage is always in the output.

112
00:07:26.133 --> 00:07:28.551
The Autopilot case also shows two Learn

113
00:07:28.551 --> 00:07:31.483
pages disagreeing about
the government clouds.

114
00:07:31.483 --> 00:07:33.733
The code follows the stricter one,

115
00:07:33.733 --> 00:07:37.640
because an API that answers
isn't a feature you can use.

116
00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:42.452
Who is this for?

117
00:07:42.452 --> 00:07:45.892
Developers who build any code
in this workbook that calls

118
00:07:45.892 --> 00:07:49.403
Graph or Resource Manager, and
the reviewers who approve it.

119
00:07:49.403 --> 00:07:53.497
You'll want to read Python or PowerShell,
and know what an access token is;

120
00:07:53.497 --> 00:07:55.927
you don't need a tenant
until the second lab.

121
00:07:55.927 --> 00:07:58.712
The reviewer's question
is always the same:

122
00:07:58.712 --> 00:08:02.053
which calls, with which
permissions, in which cloud.

123
00:08:02.053 --> 00:08:03.564
The call list answers it.

124
00:08:03.564 --> 00:08:06.962
And the app registration
itself is part of the answer:

125
00:08:06.962 --> 00:08:11.285
exactly the task's permissions, a
certificate or a managed identity,

126
00:08:11.285 --> 00:08:14.880
never a secret, registered
in the tenant's own cloud.

127
00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:23.355
Lab 1 needs only a laptop, with
Python and PowerShell, and no tenant.

128
00:08:23.355 --> 00:08:26.891
First you run Microsoft's own
snippets against the stand-ins,

129
00:08:26.891 --> 00:08:30.993
and read the test that proves the
Python credential gets no authority.

130
00:08:30.993 --> 00:08:35.929
Then you print the endpoints for this
estate, and see that nothing for GCC is

131
00:08:35.929 --> 00:08:40.467
a dot-u-s host, and that a misspelled
cloud name is an error, not a default.

132
00:08:40.467 --> 00:08:43.298
Then you list a task's
permissions from its calls,

133
00:08:43.298 --> 00:08:45.492
and try a call the task never declared.

134
00:08:45.492 --> 00:08:48.230
It's refused before anything is sent.

135
00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:55.018
The rest of the lab covers
beta and PowerShell.

136
00:08:55.018 --> 00:08:59.406
Ask for Autopilot in GCC High and
the client makes no call at all:

137
00:08:59.406 --> 00:09:03.205
it reports not available, with
the reason and the stage.

138
00:09:03.205 --> 00:09:08.286
Then you run the same rules in PowerShell:
an undeclared beta call is refused,

139
00:09:08.286 --> 00:09:13.367
and so is a worldwide address from a GCC
High session, which won't get the token.

140
00:09:13.367 --> 00:09:18.707
Finally you run both suites, seventy
Python tests and forty-four in Pester, and

141
00:09:18.707 --> 00:09:22.128
you've seen every row of
the gap table for yourself,

142
00:09:22.128 --> 00:09:24.750
with no module, tenant or network.

143
00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:33.062
Lab 2 moves to a test tenant, never
production, with fictional data.

144
00:09:33.062 --> 00:09:37.500
It uses the Conditional Access export
because it needs just one permission.

145
00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:40.296
You register the app in
the tenant's own cloud,

146
00:09:40.296 --> 00:09:44.150
with a certificate, grant that
one permission, and run the task.

147
00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:48.269
Then you watch the token check do
its job: grant an extra permission

148
00:09:48.269 --> 00:09:52.388
and you get a warning, leave one out
and the run stops before any call.

149
00:09:52.388 --> 00:09:55.463
Finally, point it at the
wrong cloud on purpose.

150
00:09:55.463 --> 00:09:58.397
Sign-in goes to the government authority,

151
00:09:58.397 --> 00:10:02.210
which doesn't hold a GCC
tenant, so it fails closed.

152
00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:08.435
Each of these dangers
has a guard in the code.

153
00:10:08.435 --> 00:10:13.872
The two that bite this estate first are
treating GCC as a government dot-u-s cloud,

154
00:10:13.872 --> 00:10:17.497
and a cloud name that silently
defaults to worldwide.

155
00:10:17.497 --> 00:10:21.214
The most dangerous is a
token sent to the wrong host,

156
00:10:21.214 --> 00:10:24.223
so every address, on
every page, is checked.

157
00:10:24.223 --> 00:10:27.946
Others creep in slowly:
permissions the job doesn't need,

158
00:10:27.946 --> 00:10:32.884
a call added without its permission,
or a beta property renamed overnight.

159
00:10:32.884 --> 00:10:37.718
And the subtlest is an API that answers
where the feature isn't offered,

160
00:10:37.718 --> 00:10:40.990
handing you a clean,
empty, and wrong result.

161
00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:46.090
Here's what done looks like.

162
00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:50.840
On the cloud side: an explicit cloud
name that fails on anything unknown,

163
00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:53.899
no government hosts in
the GCC configuration,

164
00:10:53.899 --> 00:10:57.360
the right authority and
scope for every credential,

165
00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:02.190
one token per resource, and the token
never leaving the cloud's Graph host.

166
00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:07.023
On the run side: every call declared
with its least privileged permission,

167
00:11:07.023 --> 00:11:09.083
an app that holds exactly those,

168
00:11:09.083 --> 00:11:13.678
paging and retries done Microsoft's
way, and beta declared and labeled.

169
00:11:13.678 --> 00:11:18.215
Next, Chapter 3 uses these rules
to write a validation kit check.

170
00:11:18.215 --> 00:11:19.370
Thanks for watching.
